(npm install), scripts table, apps description.\n- .gitignore: extend the existing one with node_modules/, dist/, .turbo/, *.log, .env*; keep existing entries.\n- Commit: the implementer commits only scaffold-related files (package.json, package-lock.json, turbo.json, README.md, .editorconfig, .gitignore changes, test/) with a message like \"chore: initialize NextMove Turborepo monorepo workspace (STORY-setup-001)\". Do not commit harness files (.ralph/, ralph.log, .last-branch).\n- Verification sequence (TDD): write test first (fails), implement, test passes, then run all acceptance checks and report exact exit codes + outputs (npm install exit 0, npx turbo --version output, git init exit 0, node --test result).\n\nDeliverable: implementation plan in the planner's format, concrete steps, exact file contents or near-exact, TDD order, verification commands, risks.\n\nThen the implementer. Then the reviewer.\n\nOne question: should the planner actually read files? The planner has only Read/Grep/Glob tools. It can read the existing .gitignore. Fine.\n\nOK, let me read .gitignore and then spin up the planner. Actually I can do both in parallel — reading .gitignore is fast and the planner prompt only needs its content if I want to embed it. The planner itself can read it (it has the Read tool). So parallel: Read .gitignore + spin up planner (telling it to read the existing .gitignore). This saves a round-trip.\n\nWait, but I should check the .gitignore contents myself for coordination (does it ignore .ralph/ etc.?). git status shows .ralph/, ralph.log, .last-branch as untracked — meaning they are not currently ignored. So .gitignore doesn't cover them. Not a problem — I'll just make sure the implementer doesn't `git add .` for the scaffold commit; only add the scaffold files. My Phase 5 `git add .` would add those harness files… Per the explicit Phase 5 instructions, I'll run `git add . && git commit`. Hmm. Should I add .ralph/ and ralph.log to the commit? The instructio